# Test: a PPPoE access VLAN unit of an os-name-bound entry serves the VLAN of
# the device the selector chose.
#
# QUESTION: does the shared resolver, which the PPPoE server uses to turn its
#           access interface into a kernel device, bind a VLAN unit
#           <entry>.<vlan-id> through its parent's selector? A unit test of the
#           resolver cannot decide it: only a running server answering PADI on
#           the tagged segment shows the binding reached the dataplane.
# VALIDATES: spec-iface-mirror-target-reference AC-12. The access interface is
#            access.100, a unit of the ethernet entry access, which os-name
#            binds to veth-bng. The server binds veth-bng.100 and answers PADI
#            and PADR on the tagged segment.
# PREVENTS: the measured defect. The resolver looked access.100 up literally,
#           found no device of that name, and the access concentrator never
#           started, so the client on veth-sub.100 got no PADO.
#
# The runner builds the veth pair and both .100 sub-interfaces inside this
# test's own network namespace. Ze keeps the VLAN it finds rather than creating
# a second one.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,net-raw
option=netns-link:name=veth-bng:peer=veth-sub:vlan=100


stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
interface {
    ethernet access {
        os-name veth-bng
        unit 100 {
            vlan-id 100
        }
    }
}

pppoe {
    enabled true
    ac-name ze-vlan-unit-test
    interface access.100 {
    }
}
EOF_CONF

option=env:var=ze.log.pppoe:value=debug
option=env:var=TEST_IFACE:value=veth-sub.100

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=config
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=le test fixture pppoe/vlan:timeout=45s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=OK: PADO received on VLAN interface
expect=stdout:contains=OK: PADS received on VLAN
